    {
      "id": "antony-and-cleopatra/sceneII_3.3/p@63343",
      "type": "passage",
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [
        "drama"
      ],
      "text": "He shall to Parthia. Be it art or hap,\nHe hath spoken true. The very dice obey him,\nAnd in our sports my better cunning faints\nUnder his chance. If we draw lots, he speeds;\nHis cocks do win the battle still of mine\nWhen it is all to naught, and his quails ever\nBeat mine, inhooped, at odds. I will to Egypt:\nAnd though I make this marriage for my peace,\nI’ th’ East my pleasure lies.",
      "separatorAfter": "\n\n",
      "spans": [],
      "lines": [
